    "//#eas:freshness": {
      "description": "DEAD-MAN'S SWITCH for native builds: assert a SUCCESSFUL EAS build exists within the policy window, per platform. Exists because iOS builds errored for ~2 MONTHS (15 consecutive, last success 2026-06-12) and nobody knew until a driver could not install the app. The alerting was NOT missing -- eas-inbound.controller.ts raised a Sentry fatal every time, so fifteen fatals fired correctly and were dismissed. What was absent is a check on the ABSENCE OF SUCCESS: the webhook records failure loudly (errored -> fatal) and success silently (finished -> 200), so nothing could answer 'when did iOS last succeed?'. The fingerprint short-circuit then hid it further -- runs either matched the stale finished build and skipped, or dispatched and errored, and a stream of errors plus skips is not silence. FAIL-CLOSED: only fresh exits 0; stale, never, unreadable evidence and our own misconfiguration all exit non-zero, because an inability to prove freshness must never quietly produce success. No SKIP_ escape hatch by design. Pure cores (eas-build-observation parses, eas-build-freshness decides) are exhaustively unit-tested offline; this task is orchestration only. cache:false -- queries a remote service, and a cached verdict is a stale verdict, which is the exact bug. Run: pnpm exec turbo run eas:freshness -- --max-age-days 14.",
      "cache": false,
      "passThroughEnv": ["EXPO_TOKEN", "EAS_PROJECT_ID"]
