      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#sync:worktrees": {
      "description": "Root-level task: fast-forward every git worktree local branch to its remote tracking branch, SAFELY. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans all worktrees, not a per-package task). Fast-forwards only when a branch is purely behind (behind>0, ahead=0); REFUSES and warns on true divergence (ahead>0 AND behind>0), never force-merging/resetting, so unpushed local commits can never be lost. The autonomous pipeline converges the REMOTE branches but git never auto-updates LOCAL worktree branches, so this closes that gap. cache:false (side-effecting git operation, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run sync:worktrees.",
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#sync:develop": {
      "description": "Root-level task: merge origin/develop DOWN into the current feature branch (2026 conflict-prevention). Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only. Refuses on dirty tree; no-op on develop/main; on conflict exits 1 with the conflicted paths for evidence-driven resolution (rerere replays known hunks). STANDING RULE: run after every milestone push and immediately before gh pr create so every PR is born mergeable, never DIRTY. cache:false (side-effecting git operation). Run: pnpm exec turbo run sync:develop.",
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#bump:pnpm": {
      "description": "Root-level task: bump the corepack-pinned pnpm version for the whole repo via corepack use pnpm@<version|latest> -- resolves the version and rewrites the packageManager pin + sha512 in package.json itself (2026 canonical; never pnpm add -g, which fails on this box because the global bin dir is not on PATH and would only mutate one machine anyway). Script refuses on a dirty tree so the pin change is always an isolated, reviewable commit. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only. cache:false (side-effecting, network-resolving). Run: pnpm exec turbo run bump:pnpm [-- <version>].",
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#bump:turbo": {
      "description": "Root-level task: bump the turbo devDependency version for the whole repo. Unlike bump:pnpm (which drives the corepack packageManager pin), turbo is an ordinary devDependency, so the correct bump is a spec rewrite in package.json plus a lockfile update -- not corepack use. The pure decision core (scripts/bump-turbo.ts planTurboBump) preserves the range operator (^, ~, or exact) and refuses a non-semver target; the thin driver refuses on a dirty tree so the bump is always an isolated, reviewable commit, then runs pnpm install --lockfile-only. Replaces the uncaptured hand-edit + bare pnpm install idiom that drifted (wrong range, forgotten lockfile, no runtime verification). Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only. cache:false (side-effecting, network-resolving). Run: pnpm exec turbo run bump:turbo -- <version>.",
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#worktree:close": {
      "description": "Root-level task: close a git worktree SAFELY -- remove the worktree and delete its local branch, but ONLY when nothing can be lost. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans all worktrees, not a per-package task). REFUSES on any loss risk and reports every reason at once: primary-clone, no-upstream, unpushed (ahead>0), dirty (any modified or untracked file), unmerged (commits not contained in origin/develop). Never passes --force, -D or -f, so git own merged-check is the last line of defence. Complements sync:worktrees, which fast-forwards but has no removal path -- closing a worktree was previously a hand-rolled git idiom, not a captured op. Decision core (scripts/close-worktree.ts) and parsers (scripts/worktree-close.ts) are pure and unit-tested; only the driver touches git. cache:false (side-effecting git mutation, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run worktree:close -- <worktree-path>.",
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#deps:reconcile": {
      "description": "Root-level task: RECONCILE dependency drift across every git worktree -- the remediation half that //#sync:worktrees deliberately refuses to own. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans all worktrees, not a per-package task). WHY A SEPARATE TASK: sync:worktrees documents 'Reports, never heals: the operator decides when to install', and its wiring guard asserts the source contains no frozen-lockfile or force install, because an implicit install across 45 worktrees on a 9.7GiB box is destructive. Bolting a --heal flag onto the detector would have turned that green contract red and invited deleting it -- suppressing a guard to fit a feature. Invoking THIS task is the operator deciding. THE GAP IT CLOSES, measured: a 45-worktree census reported 12 deps-stale under SIX distinct pnpm messages with no captured way to act on any of them, and seven of the last ten commits to pnpm-workspace.yaml are security override-floor raises, each of which stales every worktree at once -- drift is the steady state here, not an incident, and detection alone leaves twelve permanently yellow lines an operator learns to scroll past. INSTRUMENT: the frozen-lockfile install, chosen for two properties pnpm documents. It never generates a lockfile, so it cannot rewrite pnpm-lock.yaml and cannot resurrect the auto-install treadmill that verifyDepsBeforeRun:warn exists to suppress; and it FAILS when the lockfile is out of sync, so its outcome CLASSIFIES -- exit 0 means the tree merely lagged an already-correct lockfile, ERR_PNPM_OUTDATED_LOCKFILE means lockfile and manifests genuinely disagree, which is a human commit and never an auto-heal. Attempting the heal is therefore the discriminator, replacing prose-matching over six message strings any pnpm release may reword. Deliberately NOT pnpm ci: it purges first, and re-downloading 1807 packages across 45 worktrees is worse than the drift. DRY-RUN BY DEFAULT, matching repair:* and intake:redrive and the 2026 remediation-CLI convention; --execute is the only consent, parsed by node:util parseArgs so an unknown flag THROWS rather than yielding a confident no-op, and a positional path can never be mistaken for permission. NEVER heals a toolchain-blocked worktree: pnpm refuses to install v11.13.0 at all, so retrying there is a guaranteed-failing loop on every run forever. Sequential by design -- concurrent installs against one content-addressable store are safe (store operations are atomic) but memory contention on a 9.7GiB box is not, per the same SSOT that pins broad gates to concurrency 1. Every child is bounded at 300s with SIGTERM; one worktree failing records its outcome and the sweep continues. Graded exits: 0 ok, 1 divergent (go fix a lockfile and commit it), 2 usage, 3 failed (investigate the tool or network and re-run) -- failed DOMINATES divergent because a sweep that failed partway is untrustworthy. Pure cores (deps-reconcile.ts, deps-reconcile-runner.ts with an INJECTED spawn, deps-reconcile-cli.ts argv/target assembly) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts; the probe itself is the shared adapter worktree-deps-probe.ts, which sync:worktrees also wraps. cache:false (side-effecting install, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run deps:reconcile [-- <worktree-path>] [-- --execute].",
